= RPG Backgrounds

Choose a character profile to play -- or use these as inspiration for
your own Shepard. Each profile covers the choices you make at character
creation: origin, psychological profile, and class. No story decisions
are prescribed.

The profiles are ordered by difficulty, from easiest to hardest. Each
includes a class breakdown: what the class does, how hard it is to play,
and what kind of player would enjoy it.

== The Steady Hand -- Soldier

#callout("info")[
  A newcomer-friendly profile built around straightforward combat and a
  heroic background. Ideal for a first Mass Effect playthrough.
]

*First name.* Alex

*Origin.* Earthborn. You grew up on the streets of Earth's megacities,
learning to survive through grit and instinct. You enlisted in the
Alliance to escape that life and never looked back. The streets taught
you to read people, but the military taught you to lead them.

*Psychological profile.* War Hero. During the Skyllian Blitz on Elysium,
you held the line against overwhelming Batarian forces, rallying
civilians and soldiers alike. The galaxy knows your name, and the
weight of that reputation follows you everywhere. People expect great
things from you -- and you intend to deliver.

*Class.* Soldier. Masters of assault rifles, shotguns, and heavy armor.
Soldiers are durable, deal consistent weapon damage, and have the most
forgiving playstyle. You do not need to manage complex power rotations:
aim, shoot, and survive. When in doubt, shoot it again.

*Difficulty.* Easiest. Soldiers have high health, heavy armor, access
to every weapon type, and the Adrenaline Rush ability that slows time
for precision shots. No tech or biotic powers to manage -- just pure
gunplay. Perfect for learning the combat system without being punished
for mistakes.

*Best for.* New players who want a classic action-hero experience.
Players who prefer shooting over power management. Anyone who wants to
focus on the story without wrestling with complex mechanics.

*Tone.* Paragon-leaning. You believe in second chances and protecting
the innocent. The galaxy is full of shades of gray, but you try to be
the light that cuts through them.

== The Guardian -- Sentinel

#callout("info")[
  A versatile, adaptable profile for players who want an answer for every
  situation. Tech and biotics together, wrapped in protective armor.
]

*First name.* Samir

*Origin.* Spacer. You grew up on starships, never calling one planet
home. Both parents served in the Alliance navy, and you learned to
read ship systems before you learned to read books. The galaxy's
diversity is your birthright -- you are comfortable in every port,
with every species, in every situation.

*Psychological profile.* War Hero. You did not seek glory, but it found
you anyway. When the attack came, you were the one who kept a cool head,
organized the retreat, and held the line until reinforcements arrived.
People call you a hero. You call it doing your job.

*Class.* Sentinel. Tech and biotic hybrid. Sentinels have access to both
tech powers (Overload, Cryo Blast) and biotic powers (Warp, Throw), plus
the Tech Armor ability that provides damage resistance and can be
detonated for area damage. You have a tool for every enemy type and
every combat scenario.

*Difficulty.* Medium. Sentinels can strip shields with Overload, break
armor with Warp, control crowds with Throw, and stay alive with Tech
Armor. The class is forgiving enough for new players but has enough
depth to reward mastery. You will not top the damage charts, but you
will never be caught without an answer.

*Best for.* Players who want flexibility and hate being locked into one
playstyle. The "Swiss Army knife" of Mass Effect. If you enjoy adapting
to each fight rather than perfecting one approach, the Sentinel is for
you.

*Tone.* Paragon-leaning. You build bridges, not burn them. The galaxy
needs cooperation, and you are the one who can bring people together.

== The Shadow -- Infiltrator

#callout("tip")[
  A tactical, methodical profile for patient players who live for the
  perfect headshot and vanish before anyone knows they were there.
]

*First name.* Kael

*Origin.* Spacer. You were born in transit, raised on fleet ships, and
trained on military discipline before you could walk. Your parents were
Alliance intelligence officers. Secrets, shadows, and silent operations
were the family business -- and you were their finest student.

*Psychological profile.* Sole Survivor. On Akuze, you were the only
one who walked away. You do not talk about what happened in the dark,
what you heard over the radio, or how you survived when everyone else
died. The experience left you with a cold, analytical detachment.
Sentiment gets people killed. Precision keeps you alive.

*Class.* Infiltrator. Tech and combat hybrid focused on sniper rifles
and stealth. The Tactical Cloak ability lets you turn invisible for
repositioning or a devastating bonus-damage sniper shot. Tech powers
like Overload and Sabotage let you strip defenses and disable enemy
weapons.

*Difficulty.* Medium. Infiltrators have high single-target damage but
are fragile in close combat. Tactical Cloak is your lifeline -- use it
to escape bad situations, line up headshots, or revive fallen squadmates
unseen. Requires good aim, good positioning, and the patience to let
enemies walk into your crosshairs.

*Best for.* Players who enjoy sniping, stealth, and picking apart enemy
formations from a distance. If you like methodical, high-damage
gameplay where each shot matters, the Infiltrator is your class. Less
suited for players who want to charge in guns blazing.

*Tone.* Neutral, pragmatic. You do what works. Ideology is a luxury for
people who have not stared into the dark and found something staring
back.

== The Unbroken -- Adept

#callout("tip")[
  A powerful but fragile profile for players who want to control the
  battlefield with dark energy and gravity itself. Master the combo
  system and the galaxy bends to your will.
]

*First name.* Zara

*Origin.* Colonist. You grew up on Mindoir, an idyllic frontier colony --
until the Batarian slavers came. You were sixteen when you watched your
family die and your home burn. The Alliance picked up your distress call,
but they arrived too late. You enlisted the day you turned eighteen.

*Psychological profile.* Ruthless. On Torfan, you made the call that
others could not: sacrifice the few to save the many. The mission
succeeded. The reports called it a massacre. You call it a choice.
You do not lose sleep over hard decisions -- you lose sleep over the
ones you did not have the courage to make.

*Class.* Adept. Masters of dark energy and gravity itself. Adepts use
biotic powers like Singularity, Warp, and Pull to control crowds and
deal massive combo damage. You lift enemies out of cover, suspend them
in the air, and detonate them with Warp for room-clearing biotic
explosions.

*Difficulty.* Harder. Adepts are fragile -- light armor only, and you
rely entirely on powers for damage. Enemies with shields or barriers
cannot be lifted, so you must coordinate with squadmates to strip
defenses before your biotics can work. Rewards positioning, timing,
and deep understanding of the primer/detonator combo system. When
you can throw an enemy into orbit with your mind, who needs a gun?

*Best for.* Players who enjoy power-focused, crowd-control gameplay.
If you want to feel like a biotic god -- lifting, throwing, and
exploding your enemies -- the Adept is for you. Less suited for players
who just want to shoot things. Not recommended for a first playthrough
unless you are comfortable managing fragile characters.

*Tone.* Renegade-leaning. You get the job done. You do not have time
for bureaucratic niceties or moral hand-wringing. The Reapers are
coming, and the galaxy's survival matters more than anyone's feelings.
But somewhere underneath the pragmatism, the girl from Mindoir still
remembers what it felt like to be abandoned -- and she will not let it
happen to anyone else.

== The Tinkerer -- Engineer

#callout("tip")[
  A cerebral, support-focused profile for players who would rather
  outsmart the enemy than outgun them. Deploy drones, hack synthetics,
  and let your tools do the talking.
]

*First name.* Renn

*Origin.* Earthborn. The megacities' underbelly taught you that the
weak get crushed. You learned to survive not with muscle but with
cleverness -- rewiring security grids, bypassing locks, and turning
the city's own systems against those who hunted you. The Alliance
offered a way out. You took it.

*Psychological profile.* Sole Survivor. You were the tech specialist
of your unit, the one who kept the equipment running and the comms
open. When the mission collapsed, your squad looked to you for a
miracle. You gave them one -- and it cost you everything. You do not
pray to gods. You trust in circuits, logic, and the certainty that
every problem has a solution if you are smart enough to find it.

*Class.* Engineer. Pure tech specialist. Engineers deploy combat drones
and turrets, hack synthetic enemies to fight for you, and debuff foes
with powers like Overload, Incinerate, and Cryo Blast. Your drone draws
enemy fire and harasses targets while you support from range.

*Difficulty.* Harder. Engineers have light armor and limited weapon
options, but you have an answer for every enemy type: Overload shreds
shields, Incinerate melts armor, and Cryo Blast freezes health-only
targets. Your combat drone is a fire-and-forget damage source and
distraction. Success comes from power management and positioning, not
gunplay. You will not win firefights -- you will win battles by
controlling the flow of combat.

*Best for.* Players who enjoy support roles, tactical thinking, and
outsmarting enemies rather than overpowering them. If you loved setting
up turrets and hacking turrets in other shooters, the Engineer is your
class. Less suited for players who want to be the star damage dealer.

*Tone.* Neutral. People are unpredictable, emotional, and irrational.
Systems are not. You trust what you can measure, debug, and improve.
The Reapers are just another problem to solve.

== The Charger -- Vanguard

#callout("tip")[
  A high-risk, high-reward profile for adrenaline junkies who want to
  launch themselves directly at the enemy. Every fight is a gamble.
  Every win feels earned.
]

*First name.* Jax

*Origin.* Colonist. You learned young that safety is an illusion.
The galaxy does not care about your plans, your hopes, or your loved
ones. What it respects is force -- applied quickly, decisively, and
without hesitation. You stopped hesitating a long time ago.

*Psychological profile.* Ruthless. Results matter. Methods do not. You
have made calls that keep you awake at night, but you made them because
someone had to. The galaxy is full of people who want to talk about
hard choices. You prefer to make them, live with them, and move on to
the next fight.

*Class.* Vanguard. Biotic and combat hybrid built around close-quarters
aggression. The Charge ability launches you directly at enemies,
restoring your shields on impact and setting up point-blank shotgun
blasts and biotic detonations. Your combat loop is simple: Charge,
shoot, Charge again.

*Difficulty.* Hardest. Vanguards live on a knife's edge. Charge puts
you in the middle of enemy formations with no cover. If you misjudge
the distance, the number of enemies, or your shield strength, you die
instantly. On lower difficulties, Charge is a power trip. On Insanity,
it is a death wish -- you must learn when to Charge and when to hang
back. The most exhilarating class in Mass Effect and also the most
punishing.

*Best for.* Players who thrive on high-risk gameplay and hate hiding
behind cover. If you play fighting games aggressively, love shotguns,
and want combat that feels like a heart attack in the best way, the
Vanguard is for you. Absolutely not recommended for a first playthrough
or for players who prefer cautious, methodical combat.

*Tone.* Renegade-leaning. You do not ask permission. You do not wait
for consensus. You see the target, you hit the target, you move on.
Some call it recklessness. You call it efficiency.
